-- Node: ed-refeeding (5 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Established Refeeding Syndrome: Treatment","body":"**Established refeeding syndrome: adult rescue treatment**\nNew hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia or hypomagnesemia after nutrition restarts, especially with weakness, delirium, arrhythmia, respiratory failure or edema, requires monitored medical admission. Do not treat this as prevention alone. [6]\n\n**1. Reduce the carbohydrate/calorie load**\nIf electrolytes drop precipitously or are difficult to correct, reduce CURRENT calories and grams of dextrose by about 50%, including oral/enteral/parenteral nutrition and IV dextrose in fluids or medications. For life-threatening electrolyte depletion or organ dysfunction, consider temporarily holding nutrition with the inpatient/critical-care and nutrition teams while stabilizing. This is an ASPEN rescue strategy, not a universal reduction for every mild laboratory change. [6]\n\n**2. Replace depleted electrolytes**\nSevere symptomatic hypophosphatemia (for example, phosphate 0.8 mg/dL) requires [monitored IV phosphate](#/tree/phosphorus-disorders/node/phos-hypo-severe). Correct potassium and magnesium deficits concurrently; use IV replacement for severe/symptomatic deficits or inability to use the enteral route, individualized to measured levels, renal function and local protocols. Count potassium delivered by potassium phosphate. Do not give potassium or magnesium blindly when levels are normal/high. [6][7]\n\n**3. Give thiamine**\nGive thiamine 100 mg IV before further carbohydrate when feasible, then daily for 5-7 days or longer in severe starvation or alcohol use. Suspected Wernicke encephalopathy needs a therapeutic high-dose IV protocol rather than this prophylactic regimen. Do not delay emergency glucose for true hypoglycemia while waiting for thiamine. [6][7]\n\n**4. Monitor and re-advance**\nCheck phosphate, potassium and magnesium at least every 12 hours for the first 3 days, more often for severe deficits or IV replacement per protocol; also follow glucose, calcium and renal function. Use telemetry for severe deficits/instability, strict intake/output and daily weights. Once clinically stable with electrolytes responding, re-advance by about one-third of the nutrition goal every 1-2 days, individualized with the nutrition team. [6]\n\n**Why more calories or a dextrose bolus can worsen it**\nCarbohydrate stimulates insulin, increasing intracellular phosphate and potassium uptake and metabolic demand for phosphate, magnesium and thiamine. Avoid extra calories or concentrated dextrose for confusion when glucose is adequate; they can deepen the electrolyte disturbance. Treat documented hypoglycemia promptly, then account for that glucose in the feeding plan. [6][7]\n\n**Why diuresis alone is not treatment**\nRefeeding-related sodium/water retention and cardiac dysfunction need reassessment of nutrition and fluid delivery. Loop/thiazide diuretics can worsen potassium and magnesium losses and do not correct the metabolic cause. Avoid reflex diuresis for peripheral edema alone. Pulmonary edema or clinically significant overload may still require carefully monitored diuresis alongside electrolyte replacement and treatment of the cause. [7]\n\n[Prevention / starting nutrition](#/node/ed-refeeding-protocol)","citation":[6,7],"next":"ed-risk-strat","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"refeeding-risk","label":"Refeeding Risk Calculator"}],"summary":"Established refeeding: reduce calories/dextrose 50% for rapidly falling or refractory electrolytes; IV replacement, thiamine and monitoring.","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'ed-refeeding' AND tree_id = 'eating-disorders';

-- Node: ed-refeeding-protocol (3 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":4,"title":"Refeeding Protocol - Start Low, Go Slow","body":"**Safe refeeding initiation** prevents refeeding syndrome [1][4].\n\n[Refeeding Risk Calculator](#/calculator/refeeding-risk)\n\n**Before Starting:**\n- Correct K+ >3.0, Mg2+ >1.8, Phos >2.0\n- Give **Thiamine 100-200 mg IV** (prevents Wernicke)\n- Baseline ECG, electrolytes, glucose\n\n**Initial Caloric Prescription:**\n\n| Risk Level | Starting Calories | Advancement |\n|------------|------------------|-------------|\n| Extreme (BMI <13) | 5-10 kcal/kg/day | +5 kcal/kg q2-3 days |\n| High Risk | 10-15 kcal/kg/day | +200 kcal/day |\n| Moderate Risk | 15-20 kcal/kg/day | +200 kcal/day |\n\n**Route:**\n- Oral feeding preferred if patient is cooperative\n- NG tube if refusing or unable to meet caloric goals\n- TPN rarely needed (higher refeeding risk)\n\n**Monitoring During Refeeding:**\n- **Electrolytes:** q6-12h for 72h, then daily\n- **Glucose:** q6h initially (hypoglycemia common)\n- **Weights:** Daily (expect initial fluid retention)\n- **I/Os:** Strict - watch for fluid overload\n- **ECG:** Daily until QTc normalizes\n\n**Red Flags During Refeeding:**\n- Rapid/refractory electrolyte decline = [established refeeding rescue](#/node/ed-refeeding); reduce current calories/dextrose about 50% per ASPEN [6]\n- New arrhythmia, heart failure or organ dysfunction = urgent monitored stabilization; consider holding nutrition temporarily [6]","citation":[1,4,6],"next":"ed-cardiac-monitoring","treatment":{"firstLine":{"drug":"Thiamine (Vitamin B1)","dose":"100-200 mg","route":"IV","frequency":"Daily","duration":"5-7 days or longer if indicated","notes":"Give before carbohydrate when feasible; never delay emergency glucose for hypoglycemia. Suspected Wernicke encephalopathy requires a therapeutic high-dose IV protocol."},"alternative":{"drug":"Multivitamin + Minerals","dose":"Standard multivitamin daily + Zinc 15 mg + Vitamin D","route":"PO","frequency":"Daily","duration":"Throughout hospitalization and outpatient","notes":"Nutritional deficiencies are universal. Include zinc for appetite."},"monitoring":"Electrolytes q6-12h x 72h, daily weights, strict I/Os, daily ECG."},"calculatorLinks":[{"id":"refeeding-risk","label":"Refeeding Risk Calculator"}],"summary":"Start 10-20 kcal/kg/day, advance slowly over 5-7 days, replace phosphate aggressively","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'ed-refeeding-protocol' AND tree_id = 'eating-disorders';

